INSTALLED LOCATION
------------------
When qbm-http is installed (cmake --install), this file and the license text
referenced below are copied to:

    <prefix>/share/licenses/qbm-http/

so a downstream redistributor can discharge their own notice obligations from the
installed tree alone, without going back to the source repository.


1. llhttp
   ------
   Upstream    https://github.com/nodejs/llhttp
   Author      Fedor Indutny
   Version     upstream commit 8371b40077c0177d743bb3dfed7e2b56cba18328 (2024-08-27,
               "Added support for RTSP and ICE response and the on_protocol_complete
               callback", #477), between the v9.2.1 and v9.3.0 releases. Its package
               version was still 9.2.1, which is what http_VERSION_MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH in
               src/qbm/http/vendor/llhttp.h say -- the parser is NOT the 9.2.1 release.
   License     MIT
   Vendored    not-qb/llhttp/   (sources, compiled into libqbm-http itself)
               src/qbm/http/vendor/llhttp.h  (the single public header)
   Installed   the compiled parser inside libqbm-http, and the public header at
               <prefix>/include/qbm/http/vendor/llhttp.h
   License at  <prefix>/share/licenses/qbm-http/third-party/llhttp/LICENSE-MIT
               and in-header — every vendored llhttp file, including the installed
               public header, now opens with the full MIT notice.

   This is a FORK, not a swappable dependency: it is llhttp regenerated with the
   `http_` symbol prefix (http_t, http_errno, http_init/http_execute) so it cannot
   collide with, or be substituted by, a system llhttp. The parser logic is
   upstream's, which is why upstream's MIT notice and Fedor Indutny's copyright apply
   to it unchanged. Against upstream at that commit, the vendored files differ by
   exactly:

     - the prefix: every llhttp_ / llhttp__ / LLHTTP_ identifier is http_ / http__ /
       http_, and the word "llhttp" reads "http" in ten comment lines of each of
       llhttp.h and include/api.h;
     - the MIT notice that opens every file;
     - src/qbm/http/vendor/llhttp.h: `HTTP_UNINITIALIZED = -1` in enum http_method,
       the value qb::http::Method::UNINITIALIZED stands on (src/qbm/http/types.h);
     - not-qb/llhttp/src/api.c: the wasm settings table, compiled for __wasm__ only,
       uses designated initializers -- upstream's api.c reads the same since commit
       dc5dc9a, released in 9.3.0;
     - not-qb/llhttp/src/llhttp.c: a trailing HTAB after the Content-Length digits or
       after `chunked` is optional whitespace, as a trailing SP already was
       (RFC 9110 §5.6.3, Huly QB-555) -- two `case 9:` branches, each marked
       "qb local patch (Huly QB-555)";
     - src/qbm/http/vendor/llhttp.h: the link names (Huly QB-1139) -- one `#define`
       per function the fork defines, mapping its http_ spelling to `qb_` + the
       upstream name (http_execute -> qb_llhttp_execute), read before any
       declaration. The http_ prefix is Node's older http_parser's too, and two
       names are shared outright (http_should_keep_alive, http_errno_name).

   What the commit adds over the v9.2.1 release: the `on_protocol` and
   `on_protocol_complete` callbacks, HPE_CB_PROTOCOL_COMPLETE (38) and the RTSP/ICE
   protocol states. qbm-http installs no-op callbacks for both
   (src/qbm/http/1.1/protocol/base.h).

   Regenerating: in a checkout of nodejs/llhttp at that commit, `npm ci`, then
   `make build/c/llhttp.c build/llhttp.h`, then the prefix rename above. The result
   equals not-qb/llhttp/src/llhttp.c byte for byte apart from the MIT notice and the
   QB-555 branches (verified 2026-10-15), and build/llhttp.h equals the public header
   apart from the notice, the comment wording, HTTP_UNINITIALIZED and the link-name
   block; re-apply the local changes listed above. Output generated at a later commit
   does not compare line for line: dc5dc9a also changed upstream's code generator.

   Both the compiled parser (inside libqbm-http) and the public header reach the
   consumer, so the MIT permission notice is distributed two ways: inside every
   source file (and thus inside the installed header) and as the standalone
   LICENSE-MIT above.
